{"title":"Alternating permutation","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>An <b>alternating permutation</b> of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integer\" title=\"Integer\">integers</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/0/a70dad169cdc39a470a08a8fee6f6643d42c1d72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1, 2, \\ldots, n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"17\" /> is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Permutation\" title=\"Permutation\">permutation</a> of these integers that alternately increases and decreases.  For example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/0/cf0f1e03ecc369dcfb1d08a9ecc4524f6612c4cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 5, 1, 4, 3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/7/547df0aceb4b72ad71e4063778a9a0bf2fb9c1ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 1, 5, 3, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" /> are alternating permutations of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2303664ac9f0965e10eace539666415794b052.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" />, but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/1/9c11262a50793f47bad5c0e16a543de398bf56df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3, 4, 5, 1, 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" /> is not.  Note that alternating permutations are completely different than members of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Alternating_group\" title=\"Alternating group\">alternating group</a>, whose elements are <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Even_permutation&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Even permutation (page does not exist)\">even permutations</a>.\n</p><p><br />\nWe may distinguish between \"up-down\" and \"down-up\" alternating permutations: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/0/cf0f1e03ecc369dcfb1d08a9ecc4524f6612c4cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 5, 1, 4, 3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" /> is an up-down permutation, while <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/7/547df0aceb4b72ad71e4063778a9a0bf2fb9c1ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 1, 5, 3, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" /> is a down-up permutation.  Up-down permutations have <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Descent_set&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Descent set (page does not exist)\">descent set</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/1/151df2b70881967e7c906ffdb024750210f1171b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{2, 4, 6, \\ldots\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" /> while down-up permutations have descent set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/1/3617bfd3bf09f1bac1354a2d4c0580e381aab192.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 3, 5, \\ldots\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" />.  \n</p><p>There is no general consensus about whether the phrase \"alternating permutation\" should refer to up-down permutations, down-up permutations, or both.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Counting_alternating_permutations\">Counting alternating permutations</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Alternating_permutation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Counting alternating permutations\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The number of up-down alternating permutations of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is the same as the number of down-up alternating permutations of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, and this number is denoted <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/3/0d3cbf9ab1e976e485e5a8b8459491ffa0b561dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" /> (though this <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Convention\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Convention\">convention</a> is not universal).  These numbers have various names, including Euler numbers, up-down numbers, and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Secant_(trigonometry)\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Secant (trigonometry)\"> secant</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Tangent_(trigonometry)\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Tangent (trigonometry)\"> tangent</a> numbers.  These latter names are the result of the remarkable <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Generating_function\" title=\"Generating function\">generating function</a> (or equivalently <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Taylor_series\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Taylor series\">Taylor series</a>) <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Identity\" title=\"Identity\">identity</a>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/2/8424df2c12af44751dc98eea5a291e5e4a1de4dc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{n = 0}^\\infty \\frac{E_n}{n!} x^n = \\sec x + \\tan x .\\]\" width=\"201\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>One can compute these numbers via the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Recursion\" title=\"Recursion\">recursion</a>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/7/0b752d49529f6e4a15c9f95b5ef0450c544ff2ad.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E_{n + 1} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{i = 0}^n \\binom{n}{i} E_i E_{n - i}.\\]\" width=\"207\" height=\"53\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_of_these_formulas\">Proof of these formulas</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Alternating_permutation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof of these formulas\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Alternating_permutation&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017150958\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.016 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.029 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 94/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 310/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 2616/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.383      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.383      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:8309:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017150958 and revision id 32570. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}